---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import ancestors from "../data/ancestors.json";
const redacted = ancestors.filter((a) => a.living).length;
---
<Base
  title="Method"
  eyebrow="How to read this archive"
  dek="Every claim on this site carries its confidence. A thing that is documented says so; a thing that is family tradition says that too, and is kept anyway, because pretending otherwise is how family myths become family history.">

  <section class="blk">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">The five labels</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="chip doc">documented</span></td>
              <td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan itself.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="chip inf">inferred</span></td>
              <td>A reasoned conclusion drawn from documented facts, with the reasoning written out
                  in full so that it can be overturned by anyone who finds it wanting.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="chip dis">superseded</span></td>
              <td>Asserted in the family record, and now displaced by a document that says otherwise.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="chip dis">disputed</span></td>
              <td>Asserted in the family record but unsupported, or contradicted, by what can be seen.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="chip lore">family lore</span></td>
              <td>Told, remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious; labelled because
                  pretending otherwise is how family myths become family history.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Living people</h2>
    <p class="lede"><strong>Living people are omitted from this build entirely</strong> — not hidden,
    not gated behind a login, not present in the output at all.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The rule is applied once, in code, at the boundary between the
    research data and the published site — so a person cannot be living on one page and dead on
    another.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Judging people only by their own dates does not work on a tree this
    size, because thousands of them have no dates at all: medieval nobility, unnamed spouses,
    half-remembered great-aunts. Treating all of those as living is safe but useless — it would
    withhold the fourteenth century. So instead the build computes, for every person, an
    <strong>upper bound on their birth year</strong>: the latest year they could possibly have been
    born, given their own recorded events, their children's births, their marriages and their
    parents' bounds. Somebody is published only when <em>even that latest possible birth</em> is more
    than a hundred years ago.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Every step of that reasoning is a genuine bound rather than a guess —
    a parent is at least fourteen years older than their child and at most sixty; a person existed at
    their own recorded events. A <em>recorded</em> birth is never overridden by inference, because an
    inference that made a living person look older would publish them. Where nothing whatever is
    known, the person is presumed living.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">On this build the rule judges <strong>2,034</strong> of the 15,643
    people in the tree to be living, and removes <strong>{redacted}</strong> from the ancestor set.
    Where a living person sits between two published generations, the line is shown as continuing but
    the person is not named — and a living spouse, parent or child is not named on anybody else's
    page either.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>A removal request is honoured within days</strong>, without
    argument and without requiring a reason.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Where the data comes from, and what that means</h2>
    <p>The spine of this archive is a MyHeritage family tree of 15,643 people, exported to GEDCOM and
    parsed here. That is a <em>starting point and not an authority.</em> An online tree records what
    somebody believed; this archive's job is to say which of those beliefs survive contact with a
    document.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">So the tree is treated as a witness rather than a source. Where it
    carries a citation, the citation is shown on the person's page and marked as
    <em>attached in the tree</em> rather than <em>checked here</em>. Where this archive has gone to
    the original, the page says so.</p>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      The most useful thing this method produces is not a longer line. It is a
      <strong>shorter, truer one</strong>, with the join that fails clearly marked, so that the next
      person to work on it starts where the evidence actually stops rather than where the chart does.
      <a href={u("/hornby/")}>The worked example →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>How to read a pedigree chart on this site</h2>
    <p class="dek">Every person page opens with one, and the border down the left edge of each card
    says <strong>how that relationship is known</strong> — nothing else. It is the only thing the
    border encodes, because conflating "this is my ancestor" with "this is proved" is how a pedigree
    starts lying.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>The border</th><th>Means</th><th>The card also says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Solid</strong></td>
              <td>the link is written down in a record this archive has opened and read</td>
              <td>which record — a registration number, a file reference, a deposition</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Dotted</strong></td>
              <td>the link comes from the family tree and has not been tested</td>
              <td><span class="chip inf">tree</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Dashed, name struck through</strong></td>
              <td>the link comes from the tree <em>and</em> sits above one of the two joins this
                  archive has tested and rejected</td>
              <td>which graft — Hornby or Keele</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>The count under each chart is the point of the chart.</strong> It says how many of the
      relationships drawn are written in a record and how many are not.
      <strong>Of 473 charts on this site, 19 contain a single solid edge.</strong> A reader who takes
      nothing else from this archive should take that: a family tree is a set of assertions, and
      until somebody opens a document, that is all it is.
      <a href={u("/register/")}>The register, where the same split is counted →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p class="dek">A correction is the most valuable thing anyone can send this archive. If something here is
    wrong — a date, a place, a join, a person who should not be published — say so, and it will be
    changed and the change recorded.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
